  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1229 AM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nOverall, most significant changes to extended forecast over the past \n24 hrs have been to push the path of the ejecting system slightly \nfurther to the north, and delaying the cold front for eastern CO \nuntil Tue morning instead of Mon night.\n\nSaturday...The Pacific low pressure trough digs further south along \nthe West Coast and absorbs the remnants of Hurricane Rachel. Deep \nsouthwest flow aloft develops across the Four Corners, feeding this \nmoisture into western CO and dramatically increasing precipitation \nchances and coverage starting in the afternoon and lasting through \nthe night. Meanwhile, Tropical Storm Simon starts to push north \nalong the Mexico coastline. Forecast QPF totals through Saturday \ntotal approximately around .4 of an inch across the southwest mts, \nwhile the rest of the Divide receives lesser amounts. While the high \nvalleys will cool a couple degrees due to increasing cloud cover and \nprecip chances, the eastern plains will remain hot. Look for highs \nin the 60s to around 70F for the high valleys, and 80s to around 90F \nonce again for the plains.\n\nSunday and Monday...Strong Pacific low system stays anchored along \nthe CA coast Sunday through the first half of Monday, keeping strong \nsouthwest flow aloft over the Four Corners. The system finally makes \na push onshore Monday night. At the same time, the remnants of Simon \nslide north along the Mexico coastline and get absorbed with the \nflow aloft and pushed into CO, continuing rainfall across all of the \nhigher terrain but mainly focusing along the Continental Divide and \nthe southern mts. Forecast QPF shows a bullseye across the eastern \nSan Juan Mts, especially on Sunday, of around an inch of additional \nwater, so a Flash Flood Watch is still not ruled out for Sunday into \nMonday. As for temps, the plains will remain hot and above normal. \nFor the high valleys, Sunday will still be a bit warm with readings \nin the mid 60s to lower 70s, then cooling on Monday with highs in \nthe 60s.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...A very active weather pattern through mid \nweek, as the upper low tracks across CA, NV and UT on Tues, then CO \nand towards the Dakotas on Wed. The forecast path of the system is a \nbit further north than previous solutions, which results in timing \nchanges for cooling as well as the potential for precipitation to \nspread from the mts to across the plains. First, a cold front is now \nexpected to drop south across eastern CO Tue morning, so anticipated \nhigh temps on Tue are not as cool as previously thought. Second, \ntemps are not expected to cool enough across the higher terrain for \nthe switch over to snow until late Mon night into Tue, so the time \nframe for accumulating snow is from after midnight Mon night into \nWed. Finally, isolated to scattered showers spread to the eastern \nplains Tue evening through Wed as the upper system continue pushing \nto the northeast. There are still a few days to watch how this \nsystem progresses, but winds may get fairly strong and gusty Wed as \nthe system passes.\n\nThursday...The upper low pressure system continues to the northeast \nout of the region, with brisk northwest flow then settling in across \nthe Rocky Mt region. Long range models trend towards much cooler \ntemperatures on Thu and into the upcoming weekend. Moore",
